WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
Part 4. You will hear part of a science lecture about different sources of rubber.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.840
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:09.279
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.959
Much of the world now lives in an industrial civilisation,

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:23.120
but this has only become possible because we have the necessary natural resources.

00:01:23.919 --> 00:01:29.199
There are three types of natural resource without which industry could not exist.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:36.959
One of these is metal. Without that, we'd have no machines and no transportation.

00:01:37.919 --> 00:01:42.639
Another is fossil fuels, which we need to power those machines.

00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:50.080
But there's a third resource that's essential to connect the different parts of a machine together

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.839
with belts and pipes and shock absorbers, and that is rubber.

00:01:56.639 --> 00:02:03.519
It's now used in over 40,000 products, from waterproof footwear to surgical gloves.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.039
At present, we have two types of rubber in common use.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:15.440
One is natural rubber, which nearly all comes from the Pará rubber tree.

00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:24.080
This was originally native to Brazil, but is now cultivated on plantations in Southeast Asia.

00:02:25.039 --> 00:02:31.919
Recently, however, concern's been growing that supplies may soon be insufficient for the world's

00:02:31.919 --> 00:02:38.000
needs. So what exactly is limiting the supply of natural rubber?

00:02:39.279 --> 00:02:44.160
Well, for one thing, rubber trees don't just spring up overnight.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.000
It can take 8 to 10 years for a tree to start producing rubber,

00:02:50.559 --> 00:02:56.320
so cultivating them's a slow process. And this leads to another problem.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:04.320
With most crops, farmers don't have to think very far ahead, so they can easily change what

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:11.440
crop they produce or how much of a crop they produce if they find the demand for that crop

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:18.800
is rising or falling. But if you have to plant 8 or 10 years ahead, that's much harder.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.440
And also, the rubber tree's very choosy about where it grows. It needs the right temperature,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.679
the right amount of rainfall, and the right altitude, not too high and not too low.

00:03:34.559 --> 00:03:40.880
The result is that it can't be grown in the northern or southern parts of the globe,

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:47.279
only around the equator. Another problem is that the rubber is basically

00:03:47.279 --> 00:03:53.919
extracted in the same way as it's been done for hundreds of years, and that's by hand,

00:03:54.559 --> 00:04:02.240
by making small cuts in the trunk of the tree and putting a little cup there to catch the latex,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.320
as the rubber is called. It's very labour intensive.

00:04:07.119 --> 00:04:11.360
And it's not just the initial production that's limiting supplies.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:18.239
With other resources, such as water and glass, when we've finished using them,

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:23.839
we can recycle them. But although this is also possible with rubber,

00:04:23.839 --> 00:04:29.359
it's very difficult, so that also reduces the amount we have available.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:36.799
And in the last few years, there have been new threats to the supply of natural rubber.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:43.839
One problem is linked to the fact that nearly all the rubber trees in Southeast Asia

00:04:43.839 --> 00:04:51.040
are descended from just a small number of seeds brought from Brazil in the 19th century.

00:04:52.079 --> 00:04:57.519
This means that there's very little genetic diversity among the trees,

00:04:57.519 --> 00:05:01.359
which in turn makes them very vulnerable to disease.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:08.640
The most dangerous threat is a fungus, which destroyed large numbers of rubber trees in

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:14.160
Brazil, and which could cause devastation to plantations worldwide.

00:05:15.359 --> 00:05:22.079
Another problem is that farmers in Southeast Asia are increasingly turning to the cultivation

00:05:22.079 --> 00:05:26.640
of palm oil, which is easier and more profitable for them.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.720
And finally, in recent years, Southeast Asia, like other parts of the world,

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.279
has been repeatedly hit by extreme types of weather,

00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:40.799
and this looks likely to continue in the future.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:53.119
However, as well as using natural rubber, it's also possible to make rubber synthetically.

00:05:53.119 --> 00:06:02.160
This works very well for some purposes, for example, making engine parts or silicon pots

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:10.239
and pans used for cooking. But compared with natural rubber, it's not anything like as strong,

00:06:10.959 --> 00:06:15.679
and this means it can't replace natural rubber in other products.

00:06:16.239 --> 00:06:24.000
For example, while a mixture of natural rubber and synthetic rubber works well in car tyres,

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:32.320
only natural rubber can stand up to the extreme speeds of aircraft tyres during take-off and

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:40.640
landing. So, for some time, scientists have been looking for alternative sources of natural rubber.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:47.839
One that's been known about for some time seems initially to be a rather unlikely source.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:55.760
It's a wild plant with yellow flowers that we normally regard as a weed when we see it in our

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:03.440
gardens. But when it's pulled up and its roots cut open, they're found to contain rubber.

00:07:04.399 --> 00:07:11.760
Now, compared to the rubber tree, dandelions produce relatively small amounts of rubber,

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:19.119
but unlike rubber trees, they're very adaptable. They'll grow in all sorts of places,

00:07:19.119 --> 00:07:26.959
and they don't need rich soil. So, at present, there are several projects underway investigating

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:34.079
the possibility of using dandelions as a source of rubber. Another possibility

00:07:34.079 --> 00:07:39.600
is a desert shrub grown in Mexico and Texas, which is also...

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:53.600
That is the end of part four. You now have one minute to check your answers to part four.

